/* Set C: Paper 1 revision 2; Paper 2 revision 3 with legacy review retained. */
const COPTIONS=['I only','II only','III only','I and II only','I and III only','II and III only','I, II and III','None of them'];
const CQ=(n,stem,choices,key,topics,difficulty,...steps)=>({n,stem,opts:choices.split(' || '),correct:'ABCDEFGHIJ'.indexOf(key),topics:topics.split(' / '),estimatedDifficulty:difficulty,sol:DS(key,...steps)});
const CS=(n,stem,key,topics,difficulty,...steps)=>CQ(n,stem+'<p>Which statements must be true?</p>',COPTIONS.join(' || '),key,topics,difficulty,...steps);
const REVISED_SET_C_P1=[
CQ(1,SR`Let \(a=\log_2 3\). Express \(\log_{12}54\) in terms of a.`,SR`\(\frac{1+3a}{2+a}\) || \(\frac{3+a}{2+a}\) || \(\frac{1+3a}{1+2a}\) || \(\frac{1+3a}{2+2a}\) || \(\frac{3a}{2+a}\)`,'A','Exponentials and Logarithms / General algebra',5,
SR`Set \(t=\log_{12}54\). By the definition of a logarithm, \(12^t=54\). Also \(a=\log_2 3\) means \(3=2^a\). We will use these definitions and powers, rather than assume a change-of-base formula.`,
SR`Write \(12=4\times3=2^2 2^a=2^{2+a}\), and \(54=2\times3^3=2(2^a)^3=2^{1+3a}\). The equation becomes \((2^{2+a})^t=2^{1+3a}\), hence \(2^{(2+a)t}=2^{1+3a}\).`,
SR`Powers of 2 are one-to-one, so their exponents agree: \((2+a)t=1+3a\). Since a>0, the coefficient 2+a is non-zero. Dividing gives \(t=(1+3a)/(2+a)\).`),
CQ(2,SR`The numbers \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) are the roots of \(x^2-3x+1=0\). Find \(\alpha^5+\beta^5\).`,SR`\(18\) || \(47\) || \(123\) || \(144\) || \(243\)`,'C','Sequences and Series / General algebra',6,
SR`Each root satisfies \(x^2=3x-1\). Multiplying by \(x^{n-2}\) gives \(x^n=3x^{n-1}-x^{n-2}\), for n at least 2. Apply this separately to the two roots and add. With \(s_n=\alpha^n+\beta^n\), we get \(s_n=3s_{n-1}-s_{n-2}\).`,
SR`The initial values are \(s_0=1+1=2\) and \(s_1=\alpha+\beta=3\), using the coefficient of x in the quadratic. Now compute successively: \(s_2=3(3)-2=7\), \(s_3=3(7)-3=18\), and \(s_4=3(18)-7=47\).`,
SR`Finally \(s_5=3(47)-18=141-18=123\). This recurrence avoids expanding fifth powers of the irrational roots, and the two initial terms are both needed.`),
CQ(3,SR`A machine is selected at random: machine A is selected with probability \(2/3\), and B with probability \(1/3\). The selected machine produces two components. A component from A is faulty with probability \(1/4\), and one from B with probability \(1/2\). Given the selected machine, the two components are produced independently. Exactly one component is faulty. What is the probability that B was selected?`,SR`\(\frac14\) || \(\frac13\) || \(\frac25\) || \(\frac12\) || \(\frac47\) || \(\frac23\)`,'C','Probability and Statistics',6,
SR`For machine A, exactly one faulty component can occur as faulty then good or good then faulty. Independence for that machine gives probability \((1/4)(3/4)+(3/4)(1/4)=6/16=3/8\).`,
SR`For machine B, the corresponding probability is \((1/2)(1/2)+(1/2)(1/2)=1/2\). Weight these by the probabilities of choosing the machines: A and exactly one faulty has probability \((2/3)(3/8)=1/4\), while B and exactly one faulty has probability \((1/3)(1/2)=1/6\).`,
SR`These two routes are disjoint and exhaust the observed event. Its total probability is \(1/4+1/6=5/12\). Of that event, the B route contributes 1/6, so the conditional probability is \((1/6)/(5/12)=2/5\). Averaging the fault rates before analysing the pair would incorrectly ignore their shared machine.`),
CQ(4,SR`Find the sum of all solutions of \[\cos2x+3\sin x=2\] in \(0\le x<2\pi\). You may use \(\cos2x=1-2\sin^2x\).`,SR`\(\frac{2\pi}3\) || \(\pi\) || \(\frac{7\pi}6\) || \(\frac{3\pi}2\) || \(2\pi\) || \(3\pi\)`,'D','Trigonometry / General algebra',5.5,
SR`Use the supplied identity to write \(1-2\sin^2x+3\sin x=2\). Moving terms and multiplying by -1 gives \(2\sin^2x-3\sin x+1=0\).`,
SR`Factor the quadratic: \((2\sin x-1)(\sin x-1)=0\). Thus \(\sin x=1/2\) or \(\sin x=1\). On the stated interval, the first equation gives \(x=\pi/6,5\pi/6\), and the second gives \(x=\pi/2\).`,
SR`All three values are distinct and belong to the interval. Their sum is \(\pi/6+5\pi/6+\pi/2=\pi+\pi/2=3\pi/2\). Since we used a given identity and reversible factorisation, there are no extra candidates to discard.`),
CQ(5,SR`For real constants p and q, the coefficient of x in \((1+px)^5(1+qx)^5\) is 10, and the coefficient of \(x^2\) is 35. Find the coefficient of \(x^3\).`,SR`\(20\) || \(30\) || \(40\) || \(50\) || \(60\) || \(80\)`,'C','General algebra',6.5,
SR`Combine equal powers: \((1+px)^5(1+qx)^5=[1+(p+q)x+pqx^2]^5\). Put s=p+q and t=pq, so we have five identical factors \(1+sx+tx^2\).`,
SR`The coefficient of x is 5s, because exactly one factor contributes sx. Hence \(5s=10\), giving s=2. To obtain \(x^2\), choose one \(tx^2\) in 5 ways or two sx terms in \(\binom52=10\) ways. Thus \(5t+10s^2=35\). With s=2, this is \(5t+40=35\), so t=-1.`,
SR`For \(x^3\), either choose three sx terms, contributing \(\binom53s^3=10(8)=80\), or one \(tx^2\) and one sx from different factors. There are \(5\times4=20\) such choices, contributing \(20ts=20(-1)(2)=-40\).`,
SR`The total coefficient is \(80-40=40\). The data are consistent with real p and q: they can be the roots of \(z^2-2z-1=0\), namely \(1\pm\sqrt2\).`),
CQ(6,SR`How many four-digit positive integers have digits that are strictly increasing from left to right and are divisible by 3?`,SR`\(36\) || \(39\) || \(42\) || \(45\) || \(48\) || \(63\)`,'C','Combinatorics / Number Theory',8.5,
SR`Zero cannot appear: in a strictly increasing string it would be the first digit, which would not make a four-digit integer. Choose four distinct digits from 1 through 9; their increasing order is then forced. Divisibility by 3 is equivalent to their digit sum being divisible by 3.`,
SR`Group the digits by remainder on division by 3: remainder 0 has \(3,6,9\); remainder 1 has \(1,4,7\); remainder 2 has \(2,5,8\). Let \((a,b,c)\) count chosen digits from these groups. Each count is between 0 and 3, \(a+b+c=4\), and b+2c must be divisible by 3.`,
SR`Enumerate by c. If c=0, the only valid counts are (1,3,0). If c=1, they are (2,1,1). If c=2, they are (0,2,2). If c=3, they are (1,0,3). These exhaust c=0,1,2,3 and the allowed b values.`,
SR`Their respective selection counts are \(\binom31\binom33=3\), \(\binom32\binom31\binom31=27\), \(\binom32\binom32=9\), and \(\binom31\binom33=3\). Add them: \(3+27+9+3=42\). There is no permutation factor because each chosen digit set gives exactly one increasing number.`),
CQ(7,SR`The terms of an arithmetic progression are decreasing, and the sum of its first 10 terms equals the sum of its first 22 terms. For which positive integer n is the sum of the first n terms greatest?`,SR`\(11\) || \(12\) || \(15\) || \(16\) || \(17\) || \(22\)`,'D','Sequences and Series',6,
SR`Let the first term be a and the common difference d<0. The sum is \(S_n=na+n(n-1)d/2\), a quadratic in n with negative leading coefficient d/2. The equation \(S_{10}=S_{22}\) gives \(10a+45d=22a+231d\).`,
SR`Rearrange: \(12a+186d=0\), so \(a=-31d/2\). Substitute into the sum: \(S_n=-31nd/2+(n^2-n)d/2=(d/2)(n^2-32n)\).`,
SR`Complete the square: \(S_n=(d/2)[(n-16)^2-256]\). Since d is negative, the sum is greatest when the square is smallest, namely zero at n=16. This is an allowed positive integer and the unique maximiser.`),
CQ(8,SR`A histogram summarises measurements t. Its frequency density is the frequency in a class divided by that class's width.<table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Frequency density</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\(0\le t<10\)</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>\(10\le t<30\)</td><td>k</td></tr><tr><td>\(30\le t<50\)</td><td>\(3/2\)</td></tr></tbody></table>The mean estimated using class midpoints is 24. How many measurements are there?`,SR`\(50\) || \(60\) || \(70\) || \(75\) || \(80\) || \(100\)`,'D','Probability and Statistics / General algebra',6,
SR`Multiply density by class width to recover frequency. The first width is 10, giving frequency \(2\times10=20\). The middle width is 20, giving \(20k\). The last width is 20, giving \((3/2)20=30\). The total frequency is \(50+20k\).`,
SR`The class midpoints are 5,20,40. The estimated total of all measurements is \(20(5)+(20k)(20)+30(40)=100+400k+1200=1300+400k\).`,
SR`The estimated mean is therefore \((1300+400k)/(50+20k)=24\). Multiply through: \(1300+400k=1200+480k\). Thus \(100=80k\), giving \(k=5/4\).`,
SR`The middle frequency is \(20(5/4)=25\), so the total is \(20+25+30=75\). Check: the midpoint total is \(100+500+1200=1800\), and \(1800/75=24\).`),
CQ(9,SR`The line \(y=mx\) meets the circle \(x^2+y^2-6x-4y+9=0\) at two distinct points. Find the set of possible values of m.`,SR`\(0<m<\frac{12}5\) || \(m<0\) or \(m>\frac{12}5\) || \(0\le m\le\frac{12}5\) || \(-\frac{12}5<m<\frac{12}5\) || \(0<m<\frac5{12}\)`,'A','Geometry / General algebra',5.5,
SR`Substitute y=mx into the circle equation: \((1+m^2)x^2-(6+4m)x+9=0\). Its leading coefficient is always positive, so it is a genuine quadratic for every real m. Each distinct real x gives one distinct point on the line.`,
SR`Two distinct real roots require positive discriminant. Here it is \((6+4m)^2-36(1+m^2)=36+48m+16m^2-36-36m^2=48m-20m^2\).`,
SR`Thus \(4m(12-5m)>0\). The zeros are 0 and 12/5, and the product is positive precisely between them. The answer is \(0<m<12/5\). At either endpoint the discriminant is zero and the line is tangent, so those endpoints are excluded.`),
CQ(10,SR`A procedure starts with a real number x and replaces the current number t by \(1+1/t\). It repeats this operation four times. Division by zero stops the procedure. Which initial values prevent all four operations from being completed?`,SR`Only \(x=0\) || Only \(x=0,-1\) || Only \(x=0,-1,-\frac12\) || Only \(x=0,-1,-\frac12,-\frac23\) || Only \(x=0,-1,-\frac12,-\frac23,-\frac35\) || Only \(x=0,1,\frac12,\frac23\)`,'D','Functions and Graphs / General algebra',6.5,
SR`The first operation requires x not zero and gives \(t_1=1+1/x=(x+1)/x\). The second requires this result not zero, which additionally excludes x=-1. Its output is \(t_2=1+x/(x+1)=(2x+1)/(x+1)\).`,
SR`The third operation requires \(t_2\ne0\), additionally excluding x=-1/2. It gives \(t_3=1+(x+1)/(2x+1)=(3x+2)/(2x+1)\).`,
SR`The fourth operation requires \(t_3\ne0\), additionally excluding x=-2/3. For every other initial value it gives \(t_4=1+(2x+1)/(3x+2)=(5x+3)/(3x+2)\).`,
SR`All four operations are then complete. The value x=-3/5 makes the final result zero, but that is allowed: there is no fifth division. Therefore exactly \(0,-1,-1/2,-2/3\) prevent completion. Simplifying the final fraction alone must not erase restrictions from earlier operations.`),
CQ(11,SR`A geometric series has sum to infinity 9. The squares of its terms form a second geometric series, whose sum to infinity is \(81/2\). Find the first term of the original series.`,SR`\(2\) || \(3\) || \(\frac92\) || \(6\) || \(9\)`,'D','Sequences and Series',6,
SR`Let the original first term be a and ratio r, with \(|r|<1\). The first sum gives \(a/(1-r)=9\), hence \(a=9(1-r)\). The squared terms have first term \(a^2\) and ratio \(r^2\), so their sum is \(a^2/(1-r^2)=81/2\).`,
SR`Substitute a and factor the denominator: \(81(1-r)^2/[(1-r)(1+r)]=81/2\). Since \(|r|<1\), neither 1-r nor 1+r is zero, so cancellation gives \((1-r)/(1+r)=1/2\).`,
SR`Cross-multiplication gives \(2-2r=1+r\), so \(3r=1\) and r=1/3. Then \(a=9(1-1/3)=9(2/3)=6\). Check the squared sum: \(36/(1-1/9)=36(9/8)=81/2\).`),
CQ(12,SR`Real constants a and b satisfy \[\int_0^1(x^2+ax+b)\,dx=0,\qquad \int_0^1x(x^2+ax+b)\,dx=0.\] Find a+b.`,SR`\(-\frac56\) || \(-\frac12\) || \(-\frac13\) || \(\frac16\) || \(\frac56\)`,'A','Integration / General algebra',6,
SR`Integrate the first polynomial: \([x^3/3+ax^2/2+bx]_0^1=1/3+a/2+b=0\). Expand the second integrand as \(x^3+ax^2+bx\), giving \([x^4/4+ax^3/3+bx^2/2]_0^1=1/4+a/3+b/2=0\).`,
SR`Halve the first equation to obtain \(1/6+a/4+b/2=0\). Subtract this from the second: \((1/4-1/6)+(a/3-a/4)=0\), so \(1/12+a/12=0\) and a=-1.`,
SR`Return to the first equation: \(1/3-1/2+b=0\), so \(b=1/2-1/3=1/6\). Therefore \(a+b=-1+1/6=-5/6\).`),
CQ(13,SR`How many distinct tangents to the curve \(y=x^3\) pass through the point \((2,4)\)?`,SR`0 || 1 || 2 || 3 || 4 || Infinitely many`,'D','Differentiation / Functions and Graphs',6.5,
SR`At a contact point \((t,t^3)\), the gradient is \(3t^2\). The tangent equation is \(y-t^3=3t^2(x-t)\), or \(y=3t^2x-2t^3\).`,
SR`Passing through (2,4) requires \(4=6t^2-2t^3\). Divide by -2 and rearrange: \(t^3-3t^2+2=0\). Substitution shows t=1 is a root; division by t-1 gives \((t-1)(t^2-2t-2)=0\).`,
SR`The quadratic roots are \([2\pm\sqrt{4+8}]/2=1\pm\sqrt3\). Together with t=1, these are three distinct real contact points. They give distinct tangent lines: equality of both the coefficients \(3t^2\) and \(-2t^3\) would force the contact inputs equal, since the real cube is one-to-one.`,
SR`Therefore there are exactly three distinct tangents. The fact that (2,4) is not itself on the curve does not prevent multiple tangents passing through it.`),
CQ(14,SR`In triangle ABC, sides opposite A, B and C have lengths a, b and c. Angle A is \(60^\circ\), \(b+c=10\), and the area is \(6\sqrt3\). Find a.`,SR`\(2\) || \(\sqrt{14}\) || \(2\sqrt7\) || \(6\) || \(2\sqrt{13}\)`,'C','Trigonometry / Geometry',6,
SR`The two sides enclosing angle A are b and c. The area formula gives \(\tfrac12bc\sin60^\circ=6\sqrt3\). With \(\sin60^\circ=\sqrt3/2\), this becomes \(bc\sqrt3/4=6\sqrt3\), so bc=24.`,
SR`The cosine rule gives \(a^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\cos60^\circ=b^2+c^2-bc\). From \((b+c)^2=b^2+c^2+2bc\), we get \(b^2+c^2=100-48=52\).`,
SR`Hence \(a^2=52-24=28\), and a is a positive length, so \(a=\sqrt{28}=2\sqrt7\). The side data are consistent: b and c may be 4 and 6, since their sum is 10 and product 24.`),
CQ(15,SR`A rectangular garden has area \(36\text{ m}^2\). Paths are added outside it so that one dimension increases by 2 m in total and the other by 4 m in total. The garden's dimensions may be chosen freely, but must stay positive. What is the least possible area of the outer rectangle, including the paths?`,SR`\(36+24\sqrt2\) || \(44+12\sqrt2\) || \(44+24\sqrt2\) || \(52+12\sqrt2\) || \(80\) || \(88\)`,'C','Differentiation / General algebra',6,
SR`Let x be the garden dimension that increases by 2. Since its area is 36, its other dimension is 36/x, with x>0. The outer rectangle has dimensions x+2 and 36/x+4.`,
SR`Its area is \(A(x)=(x+2)(36/x+4)=36+4x+72/x+8=44+4x+72/x\). Differentiate the powers: \(A'(x)=4-72/x^2\). The stationary equation gives \(4x^2=72\), so \(x^2=18\) and \(x=3\sqrt2\).`,
SR`For \(0<x<3\sqrt2\), the derivative is negative; for x greater than that it is positive. Hence this is the minimum over all positive dimensions. The other garden dimension is \(36/(3\sqrt2)=6\sqrt2\), also positive.`,
SR`Substitute into the area: \(44+4(3\sqrt2)+72/(3\sqrt2)=44+12\sqrt2+12\sqrt2=44+24\sqrt2\). The paths add to dimensions, not directly to the garden area.`),
CQ(16,SR`The curve \(y=x^2-2x\) and the line \(y=mx\) enclose a region of area 36. There are two possible values of m. Find their sum.`,SR`\(-8\) || \(-4\) || 0 || 4 || 12`,'B','Integration / General algebra',6.5,
SR`Equating the graphs gives \(x^2-(m+2)x=0\), so the intersections have x=0 and x=c, where c=m+2. Between these roots the line is above the parabola: the vertical difference is \(cx-x^2=x(c-x)>0\), whether c is positive or negative.`,
SR`An antiderivative of the gap is \(cx^2/2-x^3/3\). The integral from 0 to c equals \(c^3/2-c^3/3=c^3/6\). If c is negative, those limits run right to left; the positive area is therefore the absolute value, \(|c|^3/6\).`,
SR`Set \(|c|^3/6=36\), giving \(|c|^3=216\) and \(|c|=6\). Hence c=6 or c=-6. Since m=c-2, the two slopes are 4 and -8, whose sum is -4. The zero value c=0 would enclose no area and is automatically excluded.`),
CQ(17,SR`A line passes through \((2,3)\) and meets the positive x-axis at \((p,0)\) and the positive y-axis at \((0,q)\), where p and q are integers. Find the sum of the areas of all the distinct triangles that such lines form with the axes.`,SR`48 || 50 || 52 || 54 || 56 || 60`,'D','Geometry / Number Theory / General algebra',7,
SR`The intercept equation is \(x/p+y/q=1\), with p,q>0. Substituting (2,3) gives \(2/p+3/q=1\). Each summand is positive and less than 1, so p>2 and q>3.`,
SR`Multiply by pq: \(2q+3p=pq\). Rearrange and factor by adding 6: \(pq-3p-2q+6=6\), giving \((p-2)(q-3)=6\). Both factors are positive integers.`,
SR`The ordered factor pairs of 6 are (1,6),(2,3),(3,2),(6,1). They give the intercept pairs \((p,q)=(3,9),(4,6),(5,5),(8,4)\). These are distinct lines, and each passes through the required point.`,
SR`Each triangle has area pq/2, so the four areas are \(27/2,12,25/2,16\). Their sum is \((27+25)/2+28=26+28=54\). Negative factor pairs are not allowed because the intercepts are positive and exceed 2 and 3.`),
CQ(18,SR`For which real values of k does \[4^x-(k+3)2^x+4k=0\] have exactly two distinct positive real solutions x?`,SR`\(0<k<1\) or \(k>9\) || \(\frac23<k<1\) or \(k>9\) || \(\frac23\le k\le1\) or \(k\ge9\) || \(1<k<9\) || \(k>\frac23\) || \(0<k<\frac23\)`,'B','Exponentials and Logarithms / General algebra',7,
SR`Put \(u=2^x\), so \(4^x=u^2\). The equation becomes \(u^2-(k+3)u+4k=0\). Positive x corresponds precisely to u>1, because powers of 2 increase and \(2^0=1\).`,
SR`Shift the threshold to zero by putting v=u-1. Expand: \((v+1)^2-(k+3)(v+1)+4k=0\), which simplifies to \(v^2-(k+1)v+(3k-2)=0\). We require two distinct positive roots v.`,
SR`A monic quadratic has two distinct positive roots exactly when its discriminant is positive and its root sum and product are both positive. The positive product ensures the real roots have the same sign; the positive sum then makes that sign positive. Here these conditions are \((k+1)^2-4(3k-2)>0\), \(k+1>0\), and \(3k-2>0\).`,
SR`The discriminant simplifies to \(k^2-10k+9=(k-1)(k-9)\), positive for k<1 or k>9. The product condition gives k>2/3, which already guarantees the positive sum. Intersecting gives \(2/3<k<1\) or k>9.`,
SR`At k=2/3 one root has v=0 and hence x=0, which is not positive. At k=1 or 9 roots coincide. All endpoints are therefore excluded.`),
CQ(19,SR`Find the product of the real solutions of \[x^{\log_{10}x}=100x.\]`,SR`\(\frac1{10}\) || 10 || 100 || 1000 || 10000`,'B','Exponentials and Logarithms',5.5,
SR`The logarithm requires x>0. Both sides are then positive, so taking logarithms to base 10 is valid and reversible. Put \(t=\log_{10}x\). The logarithm of the left side is \((\log_{10}x)(\log_{10}x)=t^2\), and that of the right side is \(\log_{10}100+\log_{10}x=2+t\).`,
SR`Thus \(t^2-t-2=(t-2)(t+1)=0\), giving t=2 or t=-1. The corresponding x values are \(10^2=100\) and \(10^{-1}=1/10\).`,
SR`Check: at x=100, the equation is \(100^2=100(100)\); at x=1/10, it is \((1/10)^{-1}=100/10=10\). Their product is \(100(1/10)=10\).`),
CQ(20,SR`How many ordered pairs \((x,y)\) of real numbers satisfy both \[x^2+y^2=25,\qquad x+y=xy?\]`,SR`0 || 1 || 2 || 3 || 4 || Infinitely many`,'E','General algebra',5.5,
SR`Put s=x+y and p=xy. The second equation says p=s. Also \(x^2+y^2=(x+y)^2-2xy=s^2-2p\), so the first equation becomes \(s^2-2s=25\). Thus \(s^2-2s-25=0\), giving \(s=1\pm\sqrt{26}\).`,
SR`For each fixed s, x and y must be roots of \(t^2-st+s=0\), because their sum and product both equal s. Its discriminant is \(s^2-4s=s(s-4)\), positive if s<0 or s>4.`,
SR`For \(s=1-\sqrt{26}\), s<0. For \(s=1+\sqrt{26}\), s>4 because \(\sqrt{26}>3\). Thus each s gives two distinct real roots, say r and t, and two ordered pairs (r,t) and (t,r).`,
SR`The pairs from different s values cannot coincide, because their sums differ. Every pair constructed this way has xy=s and \(x^2+y^2=s^2-2s=25\), so all are valid. The total is \(2+2=4\).`)
];
const LEGACY_SET_C_P2=[
CQ(1,SR`Let f be a function from the real numbers to the real numbers. Consider the statement: “For every real x, if \(f(x)>0\), then \(x>1\).” Which statement is its logical negation?`,SR`For every real x, if \(f(x)>0\), then \(x\le1\). || There is a real x with \(f(x)\le0\) and \(x>1\). || There is a real x with \(f(x)>0\) and \(x\le1\). || For every real x, if \(x>1\), then \(f(x)>0\). || There is a real x with \(f(x)>0\) and \(x<1\).`,'C','Logic',5,
SR`A universally quantified implication is false exactly when at least one input satisfies its hypothesis and fails its conclusion. Here the hypothesis is f(x)>0, and the negation of the conclusion x>1 is x at most 1.`,
SR`Thus the negation is that some real x has \(f(x)>0\) and \(x\le1\), exactly C. A retains the universal quantifier, B negates the wrong component, and D is the converse.`,
SR`E is genuinely different in this revised question. For example, let f(1)=1 and let f(x)=0 at every other input. Then C is true using x=1, but E is false because no x<1 has f(x)>0. Thus the endpoint cannot be removed. No property of f in the stem excludes that counterexample.`),
CQ(2,SR`Consider the statement: “If x and y are both rational, then x+y and xy are both rational.” Here x and y are real. Which statement is its contrapositive?`,SR`If x+y and xy are both irrational, then x and y are both irrational. || If x and y are both irrational, then x+y is irrational or xy is irrational. || If x+y and xy are both rational, then x and y are both rational. || If x is irrational or y is irrational, then x+y is irrational or xy is irrational. || If x+y is irrational or xy is irrational, then x is irrational or y is irrational. || If x+y is irrational or xy is irrational, then x and y are both irrational.`,'E','Logic',5.5,
SR`The original has form “if A then B”, where A says both inputs are rational and B says both their sum and product are rational. Its contrapositive has form “if not B then not A”.`,
SR`The failure of “both are rational” means at least one is irrational, not necessarily both. Thus not B is “x+y is irrational or xy is irrational”; not A is “x is irrational or y is irrational”. The required statement is E.`,
SR`For instance x=1 and y=\(\sqrt2\) give irrational sum and product but only one irrational input, showing why the stronger conclusion in F is wrong. A also wrongly strengthens the antecedent to require both outputs irrational. C is the converse and D the inverse. The six options are distinct statements.`),
CQ(3,SR`A student claims that for every non-zero real x, if \(x>1/x\), then \(x>1\). Which value disproves the claim?`,SR`\(-2\) || \(-\frac12\) || \(\frac12\) || 1 || 2`,'B','Logic / General algebra',4.5,
SR`To disprove an implication, we need its hypothesis true but its conclusion false. At x=-1/2, its reciprocal is -2, so \(-1/2>-2\) is true. However \(-1/2>1\) is false. Thus B is a counterexample.`,
SR`At x=-2 the hypothesis fails because \(-2<-1/2\). At x=1/2 it fails because \(1/2<2\). At x=1 it fails because 1 is not strictly greater than 1. At x=2 both \(2>1/2\) and \(2>1\) hold. None of these other values disproves the statement.`),
CS(4,SR`A function f is defined for every real number and satisfies \(f(x+1)=f(x)+1\) for every real x.<p>I. \(f(0)=0\).</p><p>II. f is one-to-one, meaning that distinct inputs give distinct outputs.</p><p>III. \(f(n)=n\) for every integer n.</p>`,'H','Logic / Functions and Graphs',6.5,
SR`For I and III, take \(f(x)=x+5\). Then \(f(x+1)=x+6=f(x)+1\), so it meets the hypothesis. But f(0)=5, not zero, and f(n)=n+5, not n. Thus neither I nor III is forced.`,
SR`For II, define f(x) to be the greatest integer not exceeding x. More explicitly, f(x)=m on each interval \(m\le x<m+1\), for every integer m. Increasing the input by 1 moves it to the next such interval, so \(f(x+1)=m+1=f(x)+1\).`,
SR`This function is not one-to-one: f(0)=f(1/2)=0 even though the inputs differ. It therefore disproves II. A functional relation over unit shifts does not control all behaviour inside each unit interval. None of the statements must hold.`),
CQ(5,SR`A student solves \(\sqrt{x+5}=x-1\) as follows.<p>Line 1. Squaring gives \(x+5=x^2-2x+1\).</p><p>Line 2. Hence \(x^2-3x-4=0\).</p><p>Line 3. Factorising gives \((x-4)(x+1)=0\), so x=4 or x=-1.</p><p>Line 4. Both make x+5 non-negative, so both solve the original equation.</p><p>Line 5. The solution set is \(\{-1,4\}\).</p>Which line contains the first error?`,SR`Line 1 || Line 2 || Line 3 || Line 4 || Line 5 || There is no error.`,'D','Logic / General algebra',5,
SR`Squaring an equality is a valid forward implication, so line 1 is correct for any original solution. Subtracting x+5 produces line 2, and \((x-4)(x+1)=x^2-3x-4\), so line 3 correctly identifies the candidates.`,
SR`Line 4 checks only whether the square root exists. The original equation also requires its right side x-1 to be non-negative, because a square root cannot be negative. This requirement was lost on squaring.`,
SR`Check both candidates. At x=4, \(\sqrt9=3=4-1\), so it works. At x=-1, \(\sqrt4=2\), but \(x-1=-2\), so it fails. Only x=4 solves the original. The first unjustified inference is line 4, not the earlier squaring itself.`),
CQ(6,SR`Arrange these statements into a proof that if n is an integer and \(n^2\) is odd, then n is odd.<p>P. Then \(n^2=4k^2\), which is even.</p><p>Q. Suppose, for contradiction, that n is even, so \(n=2k\) for an integer k.</p><p>R. Let n be an integer for which \(n^2\) is odd.</p><p>S. This contradicts the hypothesis, so n is odd.</p>Which order gives the proof as written?`,SR`R, Q, P, S || Q, R, P, S || R, P, Q, S || Q, P, R, S || R, Q, S, P`,'A','Logic',5,
SR`R states the hypothesis and fixes the integer under discussion. Q assumes the opposite of the desired conclusion, introducing an integer k with n=2k.`,
SR`Squaring this expression gives \(n^2=(2k)^2=4k^2=2(2k^2)\). Since \(2k^2\) is an integer, the square is even; this is P. But R says it is odd, and an integer cannot be both even and odd. Thus S rejects the assumption that n is even.`,
SR`The listed order is R,Q,P,S. In particular k must be introduced before P uses it, and the contradiction cannot be concluded before the even square has been obtained.`),
CS(7,SR`Let \(f(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx+c\), with real coefficients. Let P mean that f has three distinct real roots, Q that \(f'\) has two distinct real roots, and R that f has a local maximum and a local minimum.<p>I. Q is sufficient for P.</p><p>II. P is sufficient for Q.</p><p>III. Q and R are equivalent.</p>`,'F','Logic / Differentiation / General algebra',6.5,
SR`I is false. Take \(f(x)=x^3-3x+10\). Its derivative is \(3x^2-3\), with roots -1 and 1, so Q holds. The local maximum is f(-1)=12 and the local minimum is f(1)=8, both above the axis. The curve crosses the axis only on its increasing left branch, so P fails.`,
SR`For II, suppose the distinct roots are r,s,t. Expansion of \((x-r)(x-s)(x-t)\) gives \(a=-(r+s+t)\) and \(b=rs+rt+st\). The discriminant of \(f'(x)=3x^2+2ax+b\) is \(4(a^2-3b)\).`,
SR`Now \(a^2-3b=r^2+s^2+t^2-rs-rt-st=\tfrac12[(r-s)^2+(r-t)^2+(s-t)^2]>0\), because the roots are distinct. Thus the derivative has two distinct real roots, proving II without needing Rolle's theorem.`,
SR`For III, a quadratic derivative with positive leading coefficient and two distinct roots is positive outside the roots and negative between them. The cubic therefore rises, falls, then rises: the first root is a local maximum and the second a local minimum. Conversely these two turning points must be distinct zeros of the derivative. Hence Q and R are equivalent. II and III are true.`),
CS(8,SR`Consider the positive real numbers.<p>I. For every positive x there is a positive y with y&lt;x.</p><p>II. There is a positive y such that y&lt;x for every positive x.</p><p>III. For every pair of positive numbers x&lt;y, there is a positive z with x&lt;z&lt;y.</p>`,'E','Logic',5.5,
SR`I is true: for a given positive x, choose y=x/2. Then y>0 and y<x. The choice may depend on x.`,
SR`II demands one positive y that works for every positive x. Whatever y is proposed, take x=y/2, which is positive but less than y. The required y<x then fails. Thus no fixed y can work, and II is false.`,
SR`For III, choose \(z=(x+y)/2\). This is positive because x and y are positive. Also \(z-x=(y-x)/2>0\) and \(y-z=(y-x)/2>0\), proving x<z<y. Therefore I and III hold. The difference between I and II is the order of the choices, not a change to the set of numbers.`),
CS(9,SR`For a real number k, consider \[|x-1|+|x-2|+|x-3|=k.\]<p>I. Some k gives exactly one real solution.</p><p>II. Some k gives exactly three real solutions.</p><p>III. Some k gives infinitely many real solutions.</p>`,'A','Logic / Functions and Graphs',6,
SR`Evaluate the left side on its four intervals. For x at most 1 it is \(6-3x\). For 1 to 2 it is \((x-1)+(2-x)+(3-x)=4-x\). For 2 to 3 it is \((x-1)+(x-2)+(3-x)=x\). For x at least 3 it is \(3x-6\).`,
SR`These pieces join continuously, with slopes -3,-1,1,3. Thus the function decreases strictly until x=2 and increases strictly afterwards. At x=2 its value is \(1+0+1=2\), its unique minimum. Its value grows without bound in both directions.`,
SR`A level k<2 gives no intersections; k=2 gives only x=2; any k>2 gives one intersection on each side of 2, hence exactly two. There is no horizontal piece, so infinitely many intersections cannot occur. I is true and II and III are false.`),
CS(10,SR`Let n be a positive integer.<p>I. If n and n+2 are both prime, then n+1 is divisible by 6.</p><p>II. If n is a perfect square, then n has an odd number of positive divisors.</p><p>III. If n has exactly three positive divisors, then n is the square of a prime.</p>`,'F','Logic / Number Theory',6,
SR`I fails for n=3: both 3 and 5 are prime, but n+1=4 is not divisible by 6. A universal statement is disproved by this one allowed exception, even though it holds for larger twin-prime pairs.`,
SR`For II, pair each positive divisor d with n/d. The two are distinct except when \(d=n/d\), or \(d^2=n\). If n is a square, its positive square root is one unpaired divisor and every other divisor belongs to a two-element pair. Hence the total is odd. This also includes n=1, with its single divisor 1.`,
SR`For III, use prime factorisation. If \(n=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_r^{e_r}\), a divisor independently chooses each prime exponent from 0 up to e_i. Thus the number of divisors is \((e_1+1)\cdots(e_r+1)\). Each factor is at least 2. For this product to equal 3, there can only be one prime factor and its exponent must be 2. Therefore \(n=p^2\), as claimed. II and III hold.`),
CS(11,SR`Let f and g map real numbers to real numbers. A function is one-to-one if distinct inputs give distinct outputs, and onto if every real number is an output. The composite function h is defined by \(h(x)=g(f(x))\).<p>I. If h is one-to-one, f is one-to-one.</p><p>II. If h is one-to-one, g is one-to-one.</p><p>III. If f and g are both onto, h is onto.</p>`,'E','Logic / Functions and Graphs',6.5,
SR`For I, suppose f(a)=f(b). Applying g to the equal values gives h(a)=h(b). If h is one-to-one this forces a=b, which is exactly the condition that f is one-to-one. Thus I holds.`,
SR`II is false. Take \(f(x)=2^x\) and \(g(x)=x^2\). Then \(h(x)=(2^x)^2=4^x\), which is one-to-one because it is strictly increasing. But g is not one-to-one: g(1)=g(-1)=1. The composition only tests g on the positive outputs that f actually reaches.`,
SR`For III, let z be any desired real output. Because g is onto, there is a real y with g(y)=z. Because f is onto, there is a real x with f(x)=y. For that input, h(x)=g(f(x))=g(y)=z. Thus every z is attained, proving III. The correct selection is I and III.`),
CQ(12,SR`Define \(f(x)=x-1/x\) for \(x\ne0\). A student claims that whenever non-zero real numbers a&lt;b are chosen, \(f(a)<f(b)\).<p>Line 1. \(f'(x)=1+1/x^2\) on the domain.</p><p>Line 2. The derivative is positive, so f is strictly increasing on each of the intervals x&lt;0 and x&gt;0.</p><p>Line 3. These two intervals contain every allowed input, so f(a)&lt;f(b) whenever a&lt;b are allowed inputs.</p><p>Line 4. Hence the claim holds.</p>Which line contains the first error?`,SR`Line 1 || Line 2 || Line 3 || Line 4 || There is no error.`,'C','Logic / Differentiation / Functions and Graphs',6,
SR`Write \(f(x)=x-x^{-1}\). Differentiating powers gives \(f'(x)=1+x^{-2}=1+1/x^2\), so line 1 is correct. It is positive for every non-zero x, which correctly proves increasing behaviour on each separate interval in line 2.`,
SR`Line 3 incorrectly extends those separate comparisons across the missing input zero. If a and b lie in different intervals, neither interval's increasing property compares their two function values. Positivity of the derivative on two disconnected pieces does not supply that comparison.`,
SR`Take \(a=-1/2,b=1/2\). Then a<b, but \(f(a)=-1/2-(-2)=3/2\), while \(f(b)=1/2-2=-3/2\). Thus f(a)>f(b), directly contradicting the claim. The first error is line 3.`),
CS(13,SR`Two events A and B have probabilities \(3/5\) and \(7/10\), respectively. No assumption of independence is made.<p>I. The probability that both occur is at least \(3/10\).</p><p>II. The probability that both occur is at least \(21/50\).</p><p>III. The probability that neither occurs is at most \(3/10\).</p>`,'E','Logic / Probability and Statistics',6.5,
SR`Let p be the probability that both occur. The probability that at least one occurs is \(3/5+7/10-p=13/10-p\), subtracting p to avoid counting the shared outcomes twice. A probability cannot exceed 1, so \(13/10-p\le1\), giving \(p\ge3/10\). Thus I holds.`,
SR`Also “both” is contained in A, so \(p\le P(A)=3/5\). Therefore the probability of neither is \(1-(13/10-p)=p-3/10\), at most \(3/5-3/10=3/10\). This proves III.`,
SR`II is false. To construct a counterexample, take ten equally likely outcomes numbered 1 to 10. Let A contain 1 through 6 and B contain 4 through 10. Their probabilities are 6/10 and 7/10 as required; their overlap is only 4,5,6, with probability 3/10, which is less than 21/50.`,
SR`The value \(21/50=(3/5)(7/10)\) would be the overlap probability if independence were given, but it is not. I and III are the only forced statements.`),
CQ(14,SR`In triangle ABC, sides opposite angles A, B and C have lengths a, b and c. A median joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.<p>I. The median from A is perpendicular to BC.</p><p>II. \(\cos A=\cos B\).</p><p>III. \(a\cos A=b\cos B\).</p>Consider each condition separately as a proposed sufficient condition for the triangle to be isosceles. Which conditions are sufficient?`, COPTIONS.join(' || '), 'D','Logic / Geometry / Trigonometry',6.5,
SR`For I, let M be the midpoint of BC. The right triangles ABM and ACM have AM in common and BM=CM, with equal included right angles. They are congruent, so AB=AC and the original triangle is isosceles. Thus I is sufficient.`,
SR`For II, the cosine function is strictly decreasing for angles between 0 and \(180^\circ\). Both triangle angles lie in this interval, so equal cosines force A=B. Equal angles have equal opposite sides, hence a=b. II is sufficient.`,
SR`III is not sufficient. Take a \(30^\circ,60^\circ,90^\circ\) triangle, with A=30 degrees, B=60 degrees and side lengths \(a=1,b=\sqrt3,c=2\). Then \(a\cos A=1(\sqrt3/2)=\sqrt3/2\), and \(b\cos B=\sqrt3(1/2)=\sqrt3/2\). The condition holds, yet the three sides differ. Thus only I and II suffice; no compound-angle identity is required.`),
CQ(15,SR`A positive integer n leaves remainder 3 when divided by 11. What remainder does \(n^5\) leave when divided by 11?`,SR`1 || 3 || 4 || 5 || 9`,'A','Number Theory',5,
SR`Write n=11q+3. In expanding \((11q+3)^5\), every term except \(3^5\) contains a factor 11, so the remainder is the same as that of \(3^5\).`,
SR`Compute while reducing the remainder after each multiplication. The first power has remainder 3; the second is 9. The third is 27, with remainder \(27-22=5\). The fourth then has the remainder of \(3\times5=15\), namely 4. The fifth has the remainder of \(3\times4=12\), namely 1.`,
SR`Equivalently \(3^5=243=22\times11+1\). Thus the answer is 1, independently of the quotient q.`),
CS(16,SR`A real sequence satisfies \(a_{n+1}>a_n\) for every positive integer n. “Unbounded above” means that for every real M some term exceeds M.<p>I. The sequence is unbounded above.</p><p>II. \(a_{n+2}>a_n\) for every positive integer n.</p><p>III. No two terms are equal.</p>`,'F','Logic / Sequences and Series',5.5,
SR`I is false. Choose \(a_n=-1/n\). Then \(a_{n+1}-a_n=1/n-1/(n+1)=1/[n(n+1)]>0\), so the terms strictly increase. But every term is negative, so no term exceeds M=0; the sequence is bounded above.`,
SR`For II, apply the given inequality twice: \(a_{n+2}>a_{n+1}>a_n\). Transitivity gives the required comparison.`,
SR`For III, if j>i, repeat the strict comparisons over the finite chain \(a_j>a_{j-1}>\cdots>a_i\). Thus \(a_j\ne a_i\), so terms at different indices cannot agree. II and III hold. Strict increase prevents repeats, but does not force growth beyond every bound.`),
CS(17,SR`Let f be a non-zero polynomial with real coefficients and \(f(x)\ge0\) for every real x. The multiplicity of a root r is the largest positive integer k for which \((x-r)^k\) is a factor of f.<p>I. f is constant or has even degree.</p><p>II. \(f(x)=g(x)^2\) for some polynomial g with real coefficients.</p><p>III. Every real root of f has even multiplicity.</p>`,'E','Logic / General algebra',7,
SR`For I, a non-constant polynomial of odd degree has opposite signs at sufficiently large positive and negative inputs, because its leading term dominates and an odd power changes sign. It would therefore take negative values. Hence a non-negative non-zero polynomial must be constant or of even degree.`,
SR`For III, factor at a root r as \(f(x)=(x-r)^k h(x)\), with h(r) non-zero. Near r the polynomial h keeps the same non-zero sign. If k were odd, \((x-r)^k\) would change sign across r, forcing f to change sign and become negative on one side. Thus k must be even.`,
SR`II is stronger and is false. Take \(f(x)=x^4+1\), always positive. If it were a polynomial square, g would have degree 2 and leading coefficient 1 or -1. Changing g to -g does not change its square, so write \(g(x)=x^2+px+q\).`,
SR`Expand: \(g(x)^2=x^4+2px^3+(p^2+2q)x^2+2pqx+q^2\). Matching x cubed coefficients gives p=0. Matching x squared then gives q=0. But the constant coefficient would be \(q^2=0\), contradicting the required 1. Thus II fails, and I and III hold.`),
CQ(18,SR`Arrange these statements into a proof that \(a/b+b/a\ge2\) for all positive real a,b.<p>P. Adding 2ab to the preceding inequality gives \(a^2+b^2\ge2ab\).</p><p>Q. Let a and b be positive real numbers.</p><p>R. Since \((a-b)^2\ge0\), expanding gives \(a^2-2ab+b^2\ge0\).</p><p>S. Dividing the last inequality by ab&gt;0 gives \(a/b+b/a\ge2\).</p>Which order gives the proof as written?`,SR`Q, P, R, S || Q, R, P, S || R, Q, P, S || P, R, Q, S || R, P, Q, S`,'B','Logic / General algebra',5,
SR`Q introduces the two positive numbers. R uses the fact that a real square is non-negative and expands it as \(a^2-2ab+b^2\ge0\).`,
SR`P adds 2ab to both sides of that inequality, yielding \(a^2+b^2\ge2ab\). Then S divides by the positive product ab, preserving the inequality direction. The left side becomes \(a^2/(ab)+b^2/(ab)=a/b+b/a\), and the right side becomes 2.`,
SR`Thus the listed order is Q,R,P,S. The equality case is a=b, since that is when the starting square is zero; this also checks the boundary of the inequality.`),
CS(19,SR`Ten boxes each contain a positive whole number of marbles. No two boxes contain the same number, and there are 60 marbles altogether.<p>I. Some box contains at least 10 marbles.</p><p>II. Some box contains more than 12 marbles.</p><p>III. Some box contains exactly one marble.</p>`,'E','Logic / Number Theory',6.5,
SR`I is true: if every box held at most 9, the only permitted positive numbers would be 1 through 9. There are nine possible counts but ten boxes, so two boxes would have to agree, contradicting distinctness.`,
SR`For III, suppose no box had 1. The ten smallest distinct positive counts available would then be 2,3,...,11. Their sum is \(10(2+11)/2=65\), already exceeding 60. Any other choice would be at least as large. Thus 1 must occur.`,
SR`II is false. The counts \(1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9,10,12\) are distinct positive integers, none exceeding 12. Their sum is \((1+2+3+4+5+6)+(8+9+10+12)=21+39=60\). Therefore only I and III are necessary.`),
CS(20,SR`Let \(f(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx+c\), with real coefficients.<p>I. If f is strictly increasing on the real line, f has exactly one real root.</p><p>II. If f has exactly one real root, f is strictly increasing on the real line.</p><p>III. There are at most two real values of k for which \(f(x)=k\) has exactly two distinct real solutions.</p>`,'E','Logic / Differentiation / Functions and Graphs',7,
SR`For I, a monic cubic tends to negative values without bound on the far left and positive values without bound on the far right. Its graph is continuous, so it crosses zero at least once. Strict increase means two different inputs cannot both give the value zero, so there is exactly one root.`,
SR`II is false. Use \(f(x)=x^3-3x+3\). Its derivative is \(3x^2-3\), positive for x<-1 and x>1, and negative between -1 and 1. The local maximum is f(-1)=5 and the local minimum is f(1)=1. Both lie above zero, so the only crossing of zero occurs on the left increasing branch.`,
SR`For an explicit existence check, f(-3)=-15 and f(-2)=1, so a crossing occurs between them. From x=-1 onwards the function never falls below 1, giving no other crossing. Yet f(-1)=5>1=f(1), so the function is not strictly increasing.`,
SR`For III, if f(x)-k has exactly two distinct real roots r and s, dividing its cubic by \((x-r)(x-s)\) leaves a real linear factor. Its root must be r or s, otherwise there would be three distinct roots. One of the two roots is therefore repeated.`,
SR`At a repeated root r, the cubic has form \((x-r)^2(x-s)\), or \((x-r)^3\). In the former case expanding and differentiating gives \(3x^2-2(s+2r)x+(2rs+r^2)\), which equals zero at x=r; the latter case also has derivative zero there. Thus any such level k must equal f(r) at a zero of f'.`,
SR`The derivative is quadratic and has at most two real zeros, so there can be at most two corresponding level values k. Hence III holds. I and III, but not II, are true.`)
];

